{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788131991,"nonce":"eItNhj1mztRsVTDjALiIPiIyYdE=","message":"PROCESS CHANGE — Dorian, session 4, in his own words:\n\n\u003e \"there should be nothing on the muster for me. you take care of these things\n\u003e through agents or a future session\"\n\n**The escalation rule is amended. The orchestrator resolves; it does not queue.**\n\nWhat that changes: the previous law said a spec ambiguity, broken assumption or\ncross-spec conflict must be parked with a BLOCKED note and listed under \"Needs\nDorian\". That produced a growing queue that only he could clear, and it made the\nwhole build wait on his attention for questions an agent could answer by reading\nthe decompiled source. From now on the orchestrator **resolves the question,\nrecords the decision and its reasoning in DESIGN.md's decisions log, and moves\non** — dispatching a research agent when the answer needs digging, or carrying\nit to a future session when it needs a bench.\n\nWhat does NOT change, because it is a review of finished work rather than a\nquestion:\n- **After 4.3 (M1)** — the demo review is his.\n- **5.2 (M3)** — sign-off is his.\n\nWhat is preserved from the old rule, because it was the valuable half: a\nresolution is still never silent. It lands as a decisions-log entry that shows\nup in a diff, and where a resolution creates real missing work it gets a spec\nissue with an acceptance section rather than a sentence in a document.\n\nThe judgement to keep in mind: Dorian knows the game and the agents do not. The\nfog-of-war escalation earned its keep — he settled it AND added the blocker\ntaxonomy nobody had thought of. So the bar for a decision is \"did I check this\nagainst the game's own source\", not \"am I allowed to decide\". Where the answer\nis genuinely a matter of taste about how RimWorld should be PLAYED rather than\nhow this code should work, put it in the playbook (4.1) where he reviews lessons\nin diffs — that is the channel DESIGN already gives for his game knowledge.\n\n---\n\n\n**1. `advance until:` — resolved, DESIGN corrected (`02948cb`), new issue filed.**\nInvestigated rather than coin-flipped. All four shipped matchers hook\n`Journal.OnEvent` and halt on a discrete event; `threat` is `letter` narrowed to\n`ThreatBig`/`ThreatSmall`. So the set is coherent and DESIGN's line was simply\nstale — it now says what runs, plus the property that makes it a set.\n\n`condition` was NOT deleted, because it is not a naming mismatch. Nothing is\nemitted when a continuous value crosses a threshold, so no existing matcher can\nexpress \"stop when food gets low\" — only \"stop when the game finally\ncomplained\". And DESIGN itself records that **alerts fire late**:\n`Alert_LowFood` is a lagging indicator, `food_days \u003c 3` is a leading one. So\n`condition` is the direct answer to a problem this project already wrote down.\nFiled as **1.6 (fc287ba)**, wave:3, agent:opus, state:backlog, deps 1.3 + 2.2 +\n2.4, with an acceptance section that makes it prove it halts EARLIER than the\ncorresponding alert — since being a leading indicator is the whole point.\n\n**2. The digest budget — resolved, DESIGN corrected (`02948cb`).** \"~1–2KB\" was\nwritten at spec stage before anything existed. Measured on real colonies:\n0.7–2.0KB typical, 2.6KB with every cap saturated. Budget restated as the worst\ncase (≤3KB, ~1KB typical), with an explicit instruction not to buy headroom back\nby cutting the alert cap — alerts are the attention model and 2.6 exists partly\nbecause truncating them badly hid a Critical. Capping alerts to defend a number\ninvented before measurement would have been the wrong trade.\n\n**3. The remaining carried-forward items are re-homed, not queued.** None of\nthem was ever a decision for Dorian; they were tasks with no owner:\n- **catalog.py glyph cross-check** — only doable on dorian's box, gates 3.2/3.3\n  UX, and 2.6's new pawn glyph band made it staler. Taken by this session.\n- **`_RimWorld-Test` re-rooting bug** — a sibling repo, a known one-line class of\n  fix, carried since session 1. Belongs to whoever next touches that repo; it is\n  not an autorimmer decision and should stop being listed here.\n- **WirelessChargingMech missing on BORGES / workshop-Storefront vs pinned\n  Guests** — both are BORGES bench-parity facts. They belong in that machine's\n  session notes, not on this issue.","files":null}]}